WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.786
Slide the handle on your camera's front. The dot wheel turns, and the hole behind the lens changes size.

2
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:14.436
Photographers name holes with numbers: eight, eleven, sixteen. The biggest number is the smallest hole.

3
00:00:14.867 --> 00:00:19.625
The steps look random. So why do camera numbers go eight, eleven, sixteen?

4
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:25.887
Most people guess f/16 lets in twice the light of f/8, because sixteen is twice eight.

5
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:31.511
It is the other way round. An f/16 hole is half as wide as an f/8 hole.

6
00:00:31.933 --> 00:00:35.318
And half as wide lets in not half the light, but a quarter.

7
00:00:35.733 --> 00:00:39.211
Light comes through a hole's whole area, not just its width.

8
00:00:39.633 --> 00:00:44.911
Make a square hole twice as wide and it is twice as tall too: four times the area.

9
00:00:45.333 --> 00:00:49.573
Round holes work the same way: twice the width, four times the light.

10
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.608
To double the light, you need a smaller step than double the width.

11
00:00:54.033 --> 00:01:02.377
Which number, times itself, makes two? About 1.41. Make a hole 1.41 times wider: twice the light.

12
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:08.037
Now the numbers. Divide the lens's length by the hole's width, and you get the f-number.

13
00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:15.779
Your lens is 32 millimetres. A hole 2 millimetres wide makes 32 divided by 2: f/16.

14
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:22.372
In 1867, Sutton and Dawson wrote a hole this way: as a fraction of the lens's length.

15
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.463
Why? Then one number means one brightness, on any lens, long or short.

16
00:01:27.867 --> 00:01:37.654
Divide by 1.41 again and again: 16, 11.3, 8, 5.7, 4. Rounded, that is the famous row.

17
00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:42.839
Each step along the row doubles the light. Every second step doubles the width.

18
00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:47.703
A picture needs a certain amount of light: how bright, times how long.

19
00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:51.930
Twice the hole's light for half the time gives the same picture.

20
00:01:52.333 --> 00:01:58.089
Your camera has one time: about a three-hundredth of a second. So the dots do the choosing.

21
00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:03.847
In 1890 Hurter and Driffield measured how much light makes plates go how dark.

22
00:02:04.267 --> 00:02:08.702
Film speed numbers followed: the ASA scale in 1943,

23
00:02:09.133 --> 00:02:11.654
and ISO from 1974.

24
00:02:12.067 --> 00:02:17.657
Double the number, and the film needs half the light. Your film says ISO 400.

25
00:02:18.067 --> 00:02:23.629
An old rule: in full sun, use f/16, and one over the film speed as the time.

26
00:02:24.033 --> 00:02:28.875
For ISO 400, that is f/16 for a four-hundredth of a second.

27
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:35.305
Your small dot is about f/16, your time a three-hundredth: by the model, half a step over.

28
00:02:35.733 --> 00:02:41.057
And film forgives three steps too much. So the chart says: full sun, small dot.

29
00:02:41.467 --> 00:02:48.941
Now a sum with your three dots: 1.96, 2.88 and 3.34 millimetres wide.

30
00:02:49.367 --> 00:02:59.709
Small to middle: 2.88 divided by 1.96 is 1.47. Times itself: 2.16. About double.

31
00:03:00.133 --> 00:03:10.687
Middle to large: 3.34 divided by 2.88 is 1.16. Times itself: 1.35. About a third more.

32
00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:18.603
As f-numbers: 32 divided by each width gives about f/16, f/11 and f/9.5.

33
00:03:19.033 --> 00:03:24.187
Your chart: full sun, small dot. Hazy sun, middle. Bright cloud, large.

34
00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:28.844
Darker still, a time picture: by a window, count one, small dot.

35
00:03:29.267 --> 00:03:35.733
Why does the large dot stop at f/9.5, not f/8? f/8 would need a 4-millimetre hole.

36
00:03:36.133 --> 00:03:40.837
A single lens goes soft away from the middle, and a bigger hole makes that worse.

37
00:03:41.267 --> 00:03:46.893
By the model, a far scene is postcard-sharp across nearly all the picture at the small dot.

38
00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:52.717
At the large dot, across about six parts in ten. A bigger hole would cost more.

39
00:03:53.133 --> 00:03:56.344
Every setting is a trade: more light, less sharpness.

40
00:03:56.767 --> 00:04:03.749
So why 8, 11, 16? Each step doubles the light, and that takes 1.41 times the width.

41
00:04:04.167 --> 00:04:09.176
Look in a mirror as you switch the light off and on: your pupil is your eye's own dot.

42
00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:15.628
Try: back off, no film inside, watch a window on tracing paper as you change dots.

43
00:04:16.033 --> 00:04:21.583
Next, lesson five: how do you make plastic move that fast, the same way every time?

44
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:28.129
Every picture here is drawn or simulated. No photograph has been taken with this camera yet.

